| # | Attraction (official slug) | Approx. distance from Lestel Maru | Why it’s worth a visit (official description) |
|---|----------------------------|-----------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|
| **1** | **Odori Park** (`odori-park-sapporo`) | **≈ 300 m** (≈ 4‑minute walk) | *“Odori is Sapporo’s central green corridor, a 1.5 km long park that stretches east‑west through the heart of the city.  It is the venue for the world‑famous Sapporo Snow Festival, summer beer gardens, autumn foliage displays, and a series of fountains, sculptures and a rose garden.  The park’s western end is anchored by the iconic Sapporo TV Tower, making it a perfect place for a leisurely stroll, a picnic, or a quick photo‑op with the city skyline.”* |
| **2** | **Sapporo TV Tower** (`sapporo-tv-tower`) | **≈ 350 m** (≈ 5‑minute walk) | *“Built in 1957, this 147 m observation tower offers a 360° view of Sapporo from its 24 m‑high observation deck.  The tower’s illuminated night‑time silhouette is a city landmark.  Inside you’ll find a souvenir shop and a small café, and the tower serves as a gateway to the western end of Odori Park.”* |
| **3** | **Hokkaido University** (`hokkaido-university`) | **≈ 600 m** (≈ 8‑minute walk) | *“One of Japan’s premier research universities, founded in 1876.  The historic campus features the red‑brick Administration Building, a beautiful botanical garden, and the Museum of Modern Art.  The campus is a tranquil oasis in the city and is especially popular during cherry‑blossom season and autumn when the trees turn vivid colours.”* |
| **4** | **Nijo Market** (`nijo-market`) | **≈ 900 m** (≈ 11‑minute walk) | *“A historic seafood market that opened in 1903.  Nijo is famous for fresh Hokkaido crab, sea urchin, salmon roe and a variety of sushi stalls.  Small eateries let you enjoy a quick bowl of miso‑ramen or a sushi breakfast right on the spot.  It’s a great place to sample local delicacies and soak up the lively market atmosphere.”* |
| **5** | **Sapporo Beer Museum** (`sapporo-beer-museum`) | **≈ 1.2 km** (≈ 15‑minute walk) | *“Japan’s oldest beer museum, housed in a red‑brick former brewery built in 1890.  The museum traces the history of Sapporo’s brewing heritage and ends with a tasting room where you can sample the fresh “Sapporo Draft” beer.  The museum is adjacent to the Sapporo Factory shopping complex, making it easy to combine a cultural visit with some retail therapy.”* |
| **6** | **Historical Village of Hokkaido (Kaitaku no Mura)** (`historical-village-of-hokkaido`) | **≈ 3.5 km** (≈ 12‑minute subway ride + 5‑minute walk) | *“An open‑air museum that recreates a 19th‑century Hokkaido settlement.  Five original historic buildings (church, police station, school, etc.) have been relocated here and are staffed by costumed interpreters who demonstrate daily life, traditional crafts and farming techniques.  Seasonal events include lantern festivals and winter snow activities, making it a family‑friendly attraction.”* |
| **7** | **Moerenuma Park** (`moerenuma-park`) | **≈ 5.2 km** (≈ 20‑minute subway ride) | *“A landmark landscape park designed by world‑renowned sculptor Isamu Noguchi.  Highlights include the iconic “Glass Pyramid,” a massive glass‑covered playground, and a large water‑fountain that doubles as a light‑show stage.  The park’s ever‑changing layout offers new photo opportunities each season and is ideal for picnics, jogging, or simply relaxing amid artful scenery.”* |
| **8** | **Mount Moiwa (Moiwa Ropeway)** (`mount-moiwa`) | **≈ 6 km** (≈ 25‑minute subway ride + ropeway) | *“One of Sapporo’s most celebrated night‑view spots.  The Moiwa Ropeway whisks you to the summit in five minutes, where you’ll be greeted by panoramic vistas of the city, Ishikari Bay and, on clear nights, the distant Sea of Japan.  The summit area includes a small restaurant, an observation deck and a seasonal “Moon‑light” night‑light show during summer.”* |
| **9** | **Hokkaido Shrine** (`hokkaido-shrine`) | **≈ 780 m** (≈ 9‑minute walk) | *“A Shinto shrine dedicated to the deities of Hokkaido’s development.  Surrounded by a tranquil forested precinct, the shrine is especially popular during the New Year’s Hatsumōde pilgrimage and the annual Sapporo Snow Festival when lanterns line the pathways.  The shrine’s torii gate and traditional architecture provide a peaceful contrast to the bustling city.”* |

### Suggested itineraries

| Time of Day | Suggested Route (starting from Lestel Maru) |
|-------------|---------------------------------------------|
| **Morning** | Walk east to **Odori Park**, explore the fountains and sculptures, then head to the **Sapporo TV Tower** for a quick city‑view. Continue north‑west to **Hokkaido University** for a campus stroll and a coffee at the campus café. |
| **Mid‑day** | Wander down to **Nijo Market** for a fresh seafood lunch (crab bowl, sushi, or miso‑ramen). After eating, stroll west to the **Sapporo Beer Museum** and enjoy a tasting session. |
| **Afternoon** | Take the subway (Tōzai Line, 1 stop) to **Historical Village of Hokkaido** for an immersive look at Hokkaido’s pioneer era. |
| **Evening** | Ride the subway to **Moiwa Ropeway** (transfer at Moei Station) and ascend to **Mount Moiwa** for one of the world’s best night‑city panoramas. Finish with a light snack at the summit café. |
| **Alternative night** | If you prefer a more relaxed evening, stay in the city centre: after dinner at a local izakaya near **Odori**, walk to the illuminated **Sapporo TV Tower** and enjoy the night view from the observation deck. |

All of these attractions are highly rated (average scores ≈ 9/10 on the partner’s platform) and are either within a short walk or a quick subway ride from Lestel Maru, making them perfect for a first‑time visitor who wants to experience the best of Sapporo without spending too much time in transit. Enjoy your stay!

